1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,734
On the second of October,
twenty twenty-six,

2
00:00:02,801 --> 00:00:06,640
Nike's stock touched thirty-one
dollars and ninety-seven cents.

3
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:09,314
An intraday low not
seen in thirteen years.

4
00:00:09,381 --> 00:00:12,390
So how did the most famous
brand in sport fall this far?

5
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:14,951
This is the story of how two brothers,

6
00:00:15,018 --> 00:00:18,200
one rookie and one very
expensive decision got us here.

7
00:00:18,630 --> 00:00:20,001
Three weeks after that low,

8
00:00:20,068 --> 00:00:23,210
Adidas reported the most
profitable quarter in its history.

9
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:25,673
Everyone says Adidas finally beat Nike.

10
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:28,740
But that quarter, Adidas's
shoe sales didn't grow at all.

11
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:32,693
So that's not the real story. Nike
took its own shoes off the shelf.

12
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:35,450
And Adidas sold the shoes it already had.

13
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:39,220
Remember the word shelf.
It decides the ending.

14
00:00:39,653 --> 00:00:43,733
It starts in a small Bavarian town,
Herzogenaurach, in nineteen twenty-four.

15
00:00:44,163 --> 00:00:47,983
Two brothers, Adi and Rudolf Dassler,
register a shoe factory together.

16
00:00:48,413 --> 00:00:50,340
At the Berlin Olympics
of nineteen thirty-six,

17
00:00:50,407 --> 00:00:52,573
Jesse Owens wins four gold
medals in their spikes.

18
00:00:52,993 --> 00:00:55,573
Then, in nineteen forty-eight,
the brothers split.

19
00:00:56,003 --> 00:01:02,786
Nobody agrees on exactly why. The
famous bomb-shelter story is unproven.

20
00:01:02,853 --> 00:01:04,495
The records point to
the war, to old grudges,

21
00:01:04,562 --> 00:01:06,043
and to two wives who did not get along.

22
00:01:06,473 --> 00:01:08,744
Rudolf founds the company
that becomes Puma.

23
00:01:08,811 --> 00:01:11,135
Adi registers his own
in nineteen forty-nine,

24
00:01:11,202 --> 00:01:13,593
with forty-seven employees
and three stripes.

25
00:01:14,023 --> 00:01:18,716
The town splits with them,
one factory on each side of the river.

26
00:01:18,783 --> 00:01:21,853
Families, shops and social
life split by employer.

27
00:01:22,283 --> 00:01:24,526
In nineteen fifty-four, West Germany
wins the World Cup final on a wet pitch,

28
00:01:24,593 --> 00:01:25,673
in Adidas boots with screw-in studs.

29
00:01:26,103 --> 00:01:28,223
From nineteen seventy, Adidas
makes the official World Cup ball.

30
00:01:30,733 --> 00:01:28,223
So for decades, the biggest
name in sport was German.

31
00:01:28,343 --> 00:01:34,056
So how did a nobody from Oregon take
that shelf? It starts with a trip.

32
00:01:34,123 --> 00:01:34,440
In nineteen sixty-two,

33
00:01:34,507 --> 00:01:35,763
a young American runner named Phil
Knight tours a shoe factory in Japan.

34
00:01:36,193 --> 00:01:40,163
He makes a deal to sell its Onitsuka
Tiger running shoes in the United States.

35
00:01:40,593 --> 00:01:44,533
In nineteen sixty-four, he and his
old college coach, Bill Bowerman,

36
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:46,066
start Blue Ribbon Sports.

37
00:01:46,133 --> 00:01:50,853
Knight had run for Bowerman
at the University of Oregon.

38
00:01:51,283 --> 00:01:52,467
In nineteen seventy-one they become Nike.

39
00:01:52,534 --> 00:01:54,236
A design student draws the
logo for thirty-five dollars.

40
00:01:54,303 --> 00:01:56,513
It is trademarked that June.

41
00:01:56,943 --> 00:02:00,826
That is two dollars an hour,
for seventeen and a half hours.

42
00:02:00,893 --> 00:02:01,616
Twelve years later,

43
00:02:01,683 --> 00:02:04,383
Knight gives her a gold Swoosh
ring and five hundred Nike shares.

44
00:02:04,813 --> 00:02:08,836
Bowerman makes a new sole by
pouring rubber into a waffle iron.

45
00:02:08,903 --> 00:02:10,113
The Waffle Trainer goes on
sale in nineteen seventy-four.

46
00:02:10,553 --> 00:02:11,725
In nineteen seventy-two,

47
00:02:11,792 --> 00:02:14,098
the waffle-soled Moon
Shoe and the Cortez make

48
00:02:14,165 --> 00:02:16,590
their debut at the US
Olympic Trials in Eugene.

49
00:02:19,023 --> 00:02:16,590
In December nineteen eighty,
Nike goes public.

50
00:02:22,493 --> 00:02:16,590
So Adidas was the giant.
Nike was the upstart.

51
00:02:16,710 --> 00:02:21,226
By nineteen eighty-four,
Adidas was still the giant,

52
00:02:21,293 --> 00:02:25,163
and the rookie Michael
Jordan wanted Adidas.

53
00:02:25,230 --> 00:02:29,120
He had worn Converse before.

54
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:32,873
But Adidas didn't match Nike's offer.

55
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:37,953
Nike gave him five years, two and a
half million dollars, and his own shoe.

56
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:41,183
And a share of every pair
sold, not just a flat fee.

57
00:02:41,250 --> 00:02:45,930
That was five hundred
thousand dollars a year.

58
00:02:46,350 --> 00:02:48,100
On Thursday the eighteenth of
October, nineteen eighty-four,

59
00:02:48,167 --> 00:02:50,583
Jordan wore a black and red Nike in a
preseason game, and the league flagged it.

60
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:53,570
The rule said a shoe had to be
at least fifty-one percent white.

61
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,332
The legend says Nike
paid a fine every game.

62
00:02:55,399 --> 00:02:56,663
ESPN found no evidence it ever paid one.

63
00:02:56,730 --> 00:03:05,433
The black and red Air Jordan itself
played just two preseason games.

64
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:07,830
Then came the nineteen
eighty-five dunk contest.

65
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:13,983
It wasn't even the Air Jordan. It
was a shoe called the Air Ship.

66
00:03:14,050 --> 00:03:17,870
So Nike turned the warning
into an ad: banned.

67
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:23,270
The Air Jordan went on sale in nineteen
eighty-five, at sixty-five dollars.

68
00:03:23,690 --> 00:03:27,923
By the end of that year, its sales had
passed one hundred million dollars.

69
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:28,396
That was Nike's real formula:
a shoe people lined up for,

70
00:03:28,463 --> 00:03:27,923
on shelves everywhere.

71
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:27,923
Remember that shelf.

72
00:03:28,043 --> 00:03:30,232
The formula kept working.
In nineteen eighty-seven,

73
00:03:30,299 --> 00:03:32,023
Nike put a window in the sole: Air Max.

74
00:03:32,453 --> 00:03:38,066
A year later came three words,
written by its ad agency: Just Do It.

75
00:03:38,133 --> 00:03:42,773
The first ad starred a
runner named Walt Stack.

76
00:03:43,203 --> 00:03:44,817
Adidas found its audience somewhere
else. In nineteen eighty-six,

77
00:03:44,884 --> 00:03:45,626
Run-DMC released "My Adidas."

78
00:03:45,693 --> 00:03:49,870
At Madison Square Garden,
fans held up their Superstars,

79
00:03:49,937 --> 00:03:51,976
and Adidas signed the band.

80
00:03:52,043 --> 00:03:54,943
Adidas calls it the birth of
non-athletic promotion in sporting goods.

81
00:03:55,383 --> 00:03:57,216
Nike's real scare came from Reebok,

82
00:03:57,283 --> 00:04:00,966
which took the American lead in the
late eighties on aerobics shoes.

83
00:04:01,033 --> 00:04:04,623
But Nike took the lead back
in nineteen eighty-eight.

84
00:04:05,053 --> 00:04:10,466
Nike's yearly sales passed one
billion dollars in the mid-eighties.

85
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:12,686
They reached six and a half
billion by nineteen ninety-six,

86
00:04:12,753 --> 00:04:14,183
and ten billion by two thousand three.

87
00:04:14,603 --> 00:04:17,466
In nineteen ninety-seven,
Jordan got his own brand,

88
00:04:17,533 --> 00:04:19,773
with the Jumpman instead of the Swoosh.

89
00:04:20,213 --> 00:04:20,716
So in two thousand six,

90
00:04:20,783 --> 00:04:22,327
Adidas bought Reebok for about
three point eight billion dollars,

91
00:04:22,394 --> 00:04:23,006
to fight Nike in America.

92
00:04:23,073 --> 00:04:30,123
It even dressed the NBA, until Nike took
that contract over in twenty seventeen.

93
00:04:30,543 --> 00:04:31,553
In twenty thirteen, Kanye
West left Nike for Adidas.

94
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:32,613
The first Yeezy Boost
arrived in twenty fifteen.

95
00:04:33,043 --> 00:04:37,890
In early twenty seventeen, Adidas grew
faster than Nike in North America,

96
00:04:37,957 --> 00:04:40,306
on Boost, Superstar and Stan Smith.

97
00:04:40,373 --> 00:04:41,608
By twenty seventeen,

98
00:04:41,675 --> 00:04:46,486
Adidas had climbed to about eleven
percent of the American sneaker market.

99
00:04:46,553 --> 00:04:46,857
Nike still had thirty-seven percent.

100
00:04:49,263 --> 00:04:46,857
Nike didn't need to chase
anyone. It was on every shelf.

101
00:04:53,903 --> 00:04:46,857
So what could possibly go wrong?

102
00:04:46,977 --> 00:04:50,750
For thirty-five years,
that formula worked.

103
00:04:50,817 --> 00:04:56,660
Then, in June twenty twenty, Nike's chief
executive, John Donahoe, changed it.

104
00:04:56,727 --> 00:04:58,557
He called it Consumer Direct Acceleration.

105
00:04:58,987 --> 00:05:02,917
The plan: sell through Nike's own app
and stores, and cut out the middlemen.

106
00:05:03,347 --> 00:05:08,020
Nike walked away from Belk,
Dillard's, Zappos and other stores.

107
00:05:08,087 --> 00:05:12,077
It had already left
Amazon in twenty nineteen.

108
00:05:12,507 --> 00:05:13,041
In fiscal twenty twenty,

109
00:05:13,108 --> 00:05:15,130
about three quarters of Nike brand sales
still went through other people's shops.

110
00:05:15,197 --> 00:05:17,997
By fiscal twenty twenty-four, direct
sales were about forty-four percent.

111
00:05:18,427 --> 00:05:27,227
The margin was higher. But it
meant the shelf was shrinking.

112
00:05:27,657 --> 00:05:33,465
And the app kept selling the same
few classics: Dunks, Air Force Ones,

113
00:05:33,532 --> 00:05:34,690
Jordan retros.

114
00:05:34,757 --> 00:05:35,570
In twenty twenty-four,

115
00:05:35,637 --> 00:05:38,270
NPR reported the complaint that
Nike had lost its innovation mojo.

116
00:05:38,337 --> 00:05:42,137
Analysts blamed the same shoes,
dropped again and again in new colours.

117
00:05:42,567 --> 00:05:46,297
A Bloomberg profile of Donahoe was
titled "The Man Who Made Nike Uncool."

118
00:05:46,727 --> 00:05:50,157
In fiscal twenty twenty-five,
revenue fell ten percent.

119
00:05:50,577 --> 00:05:52,840
Net income fell forty-four percent.

120
00:05:52,907 --> 00:05:55,567
And fiscal twenty twenty-six was flat.

121
00:05:55,997 --> 00:05:59,800
Donahoe left. Elliott Hill
came back as chief executive.

122
00:05:59,867 --> 00:06:04,212
He had started at Nike as an
intern in nineteen eighty-eight,

123
00:06:04,279 --> 00:06:06,377
and retired in twenty twenty.

124
00:06:06,807 --> 00:06:06,867
His plan, Win Now,

125
00:06:06,934 --> 00:06:07,457
reorganised Nike around sports again
and rebuilt its ties with the stores.

126
00:06:13,367 --> 00:06:07,457
His first job: get Nike
back on the shelves.

127
00:06:07,577 --> 00:06:14,047
But here's the part nobody saw coming.
Adidas, meanwhile, had its own disaster.

128
00:06:14,477 --> 00:06:17,464
In October twenty twenty-two,
it ended its partnership with Ye,

129
00:06:17,531 --> 00:06:19,277
the rapper once known as Kanye West.

130
00:06:19,697 --> 00:06:20,129
ADIDAS:

131
00:06:20,196 --> 00:06:25,327
adidas does not tolerate antisemitism
and any other sort of hate speech.

132
00:06:25,757 --> 00:06:31,390
It was left with a warehouse
full of Yeezy shoes.

133
00:06:31,457 --> 00:06:31,875
And a forecast:

134
00:06:31,942 --> 00:06:34,041
a seven-hundred-million-euro
operating loss in twenty twenty-three,

135
00:06:34,108 --> 00:06:34,917
if it couldn't sell them.

136
00:06:35,347 --> 00:06:37,607
The new chief executive,
Bjorn Gulden, came from Puma,

137
00:06:37,674 --> 00:06:40,080
where he had been chief
executive since twenty thirteen.

138
00:06:40,147 --> 00:06:45,761
GULDEN: The numbers speak for themselves.

139
00:06:45,828 --> 00:06:52,757
We are currently not
performing the way we should.

140
00:06:53,187 --> 00:07:02,037
Then he did something deeply unglamorous.
He sold what Adidas already had.

141
00:07:02,467 --> 00:07:06,047
The Samba. The Gazelle. The Spezial.
Old shoes, made in far bigger numbers.

142
00:07:06,477 --> 00:07:12,207
The Samba was named shoe of the year,
the brand's first since twenty fifteen.

143
00:07:12,647 --> 00:07:16,070
Gulden later admitted
how close it had been.

144
00:07:16,137 --> 00:07:21,765
GULDEN: I have to be very honest and
confess that at the beginning of 2023,

145
00:07:21,832 --> 00:07:25,410
when the company's
performance was not too good,

146
00:07:25,477 --> 00:07:30,717
it was a godsend to have the Samba
and that they were in such demand.

147
00:07:31,147 --> 00:07:35,141
And instead of writing the
Yeezys off, Adidas sold them,

148
00:07:35,208 --> 00:07:38,030
for about one point four billion euros.

149
00:07:38,097 --> 00:07:42,280
It set aside two hundred and
sixty million for charity.

150
00:07:42,347 --> 00:07:46,547
The last pair went at the
end of twenty twenty-four.

151
00:07:46,977 --> 00:07:50,400
Its finance chief, Harm
Ohlmeyer, closed the chapter.

152
00:07:50,467 --> 00:07:53,725
OHLMEYER: There is not
one Yeezy shoe left,

153
00:07:53,792 --> 00:07:57,737
it has all been sold and
that episode is behind us.

154
00:07:58,167 --> 00:07:59,863
The forecast loss never came.

155
00:07:59,930 --> 00:08:03,510
Adidas closed twenty twenty-three
with an operating profit.

156
00:08:03,577 --> 00:08:08,400
On the bottom line, it still posted a
net loss from continuing operations,

157
00:08:08,467 --> 00:08:10,847
its first since nineteen ninety-two.

158
00:08:11,277 --> 00:08:12,120
Operating profit went from two
hundred and sixty-eight million euros

159
00:08:12,187 --> 00:08:13,057
in twenty twenty-three to over
two billion in twenty twenty-five.

160
00:08:19,747 --> 00:08:13,057
Adidas had climbed back onto the shelf.

161
00:08:13,177 --> 00:08:17,107
So who actually won twenty
twenty-six? First came the World Cup.

162
00:08:17,537 --> 00:08:22,490
In the spring quarter, Adidas sold more
than ever: six point seven billion euros.

163
00:08:22,557 --> 00:08:26,101
But it spent two hundred and twelve
million euros more on marketing,

164
00:08:26,168 --> 00:08:28,180
and its profit grew just five percent.

165
00:08:28,247 --> 00:08:29,857
Its shoe sales grew one percent.

166
00:08:30,287 --> 00:08:32,447
Nike's six-minute film, Rip the Script,

167
00:08:32,514 --> 00:08:36,790
passed sixty-six million YouTube views
in under a week, the press reported.

168
00:08:36,857 --> 00:08:40,230
It was made by Wieden and Kennedy,
the agency behind Just Do It.

169
00:08:40,297 --> 00:08:42,427
Adidas's ad stayed under seven million.

170
00:08:42,857 --> 00:08:44,710
Nike won the attention.

171
00:08:44,777 --> 00:08:48,455
Then, on Thursday the first of
October, after the market closed,

172
00:08:48,522 --> 00:08:49,927
it reported the quarter.

173
00:08:50,357 --> 00:08:55,107
Revenue fell four percent. In
Greater China, it fell twenty-six.

174
00:08:55,537 --> 00:08:59,607
Nike told investors to expect revenue
to fall again this fiscal year.

175
00:09:00,037 --> 00:09:02,180
It also announced a plan called Pace.

176
00:09:02,247 --> 00:09:06,992
It means about two and a half billion
dollars of savings by twenty thirty-one,

177
00:09:07,059 --> 00:09:08,047
and fewer roles.

178
00:09:08,477 --> 00:09:10,510
On Friday morning, the stock
hit its thirteen-year low.

179
00:09:10,577 --> 00:09:13,937
It was down about eighty-one percent from
its record in November twenty twenty-one.

180
00:09:14,377 --> 00:09:19,100
Banks cut their price targets.
Berenberg told clients to sell.

181
00:09:19,167 --> 00:09:23,580
It said Nike seemed to have accepted
a smaller place in sportswear.

182
00:09:23,647 --> 00:09:26,312
Only about a quarter of the analysts
who cover Nike still said buy,

183
00:09:26,379 --> 00:09:27,847
the fewest in at least twenty years.

184
00:09:28,277 --> 00:09:34,310
Nike's chief executive,
Elliott Hill, put it this way.

185
00:09:34,377 --> 00:09:34,412
HILL:

186
00:09:34,479 --> 00:09:35,494
Our Nike performance business
is not yet large enough

187
00:09:35,561 --> 00:09:36,637
to offset the pressure we are
seeing in Nike sportswear,

188
00:09:36,704 --> 00:09:37,357
Jordan brand, and Greater China.

189
00:09:37,787 --> 00:09:46,950
In September, Nike had dropped
out of the S and P one hundred.

190
00:09:47,017 --> 00:09:50,827
It had been in that index
since two thousand eight.

191
00:09:51,257 --> 00:09:54,120
So had Adidas really won?

192
00:09:54,187 --> 00:09:55,760
On Tuesday the twentieth of October,
Adidas put out its numbers early.

193
00:09:55,827 --> 00:09:58,511
It had made just over a billion euros
of operating profit in a single quarter,

194
00:09:58,578 --> 00:09:59,630
the first time in its history.

195
00:09:59,697 --> 00:10:02,570
It raised its outlook
for the year to about

196
00:10:02,637 --> 00:10:05,987
two and a half billion
euros of operating profit.

197
00:10:06,417 --> 00:10:11,427
About two hundred million euros
of it was a one-off tariff refund.

198
00:10:11,847 --> 00:10:15,677
And its footwear sales were flat.
Adidas grew on clothing and sports gear.

199
00:10:16,107 --> 00:10:20,187
So Adidas didn't beat Nike at shoes.
Nike had stepped off the shelf.

200
00:10:20,610 --> 00:10:23,438
Today, wholesale is Nike's
growing channel again:

201
00:10:23,505 --> 00:10:27,110
sixty-one percent of brand sales
in fiscal twenty twenty-six.

202
00:10:27,540 --> 00:10:29,908
The company behind the
world's most famous shoe

203
00:10:29,975 --> 00:10:32,100
forgot that people have to find it first.

204
00:10:32,530 --> 00:10:35,829
And the company facing a
seven-hundred-million-euro loss

205
00:10:35,896 --> 00:10:38,300
was saved by shoes from its own archive.

206
00:10:38,730 --> 00:10:41,479
Here's the lesson. The
shelf is the strategy.

207
00:10:41,546 --> 00:10:44,050
Where you sell is part of what you sell.

208
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:46,036
And Nike is far from finished.

209
00:10:46,103 --> 00:10:49,233
It has raised its dividend for
twenty-four years in a row.

210
00:10:49,300 --> 00:10:53,110
By the end of October, the stock
was back above thirty-eight dollars.

211
00:10:53,540 --> 00:10:56,970
Nike shows investors its new plan
on the seventeenth of November.

212
00:10:57,400 --> 00:11:02,080
The question is whether a company can
win back a shelf it walked away from.

213
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:04,973
So remember that shelf
from nineteen eighty-five.

214
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:09,333
Nike built an empire on being everywhere,
and then decided it didn't need to be.

215
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:11,943
Adidas simply showed up
wherever people shop.

216
00:11:12,010 --> 00:11:16,304
If Nike gets its shoes back on those
shelves, these were two bad years,

217
00:11:16,371 --> 00:11:21,463
and a company that once beat a giant will
have learned its own lesson the hard way.

218
00:11:21,530 --> 00:11:22,301
If it doesn't,

219
00:11:22,368 --> 00:11:26,790
then in the end the old company from
Bavaria may be the one still standing,

220
00:11:26,857 --> 00:11:29,730
because where you sell
is part of what you sell.

221
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:33,610
Tell us in the comments:
the new Nike, or the old Samba?

